.TH OPEN 1 "September 25, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBopen\fR \- open files, directories, applications and URLs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B open
[\fB\-e\fR] [\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-W\fR] [\fB\-R\fR] [\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR] [\fB\-h\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIpartial SDK name\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR \fIbundle identifier\fR] [\fB\-a\fR \fIapplication\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR \fIURL\fR] [\fIfilenames\fR]
[\fB\-\-args\fR \fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B open
opens each named file with the application registered for its type, through
LaunchServices, so an application that is already running is reused.  A
directory is opened in a new Finder window.
.PP
An argument is treated as a URL only when it begins with a scheme, that is a
letter followed by any number of letters, digits, plus signs, hyphens and
dots, and then a colon.  Anything else is a file path, so a name such as
.I a/b:c
is looked for as a file rather than handed to LaunchServices.
.PP
A name that has a scheme and is also the name of an existing file is
ambiguous, and rather than guess,
.B open
asks which was meant:
.PP
.RS
.nf
name?
[0]  cancel
[1]  Open the file name
[2]  Open the URL  name

Which did you mean?
.fi
.RE
.PP
Answering 1 opens the file, 2 opens the URL, and anything else, including end
of input, cancels with a status of 1.

.B \-f
copies standard input to a temporary file and opens that with the default text
editor.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " application"
Open with the named application.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch " arch"
Open with the given CPU architecture type and subtype.  An architecture
string that cannot be decoded is an error.
.TP
.BI \-b " bundle-identifier"
Open with the application whose bundle identifier is given.
.TP
.B \-e
Open with TextEdit.
.TP
.B \-t
Open with the default text editor.
.TP
.B \-f
Read standard input and open it with the default text editor.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fresh
Launch the application fresh, that is, without restoring windows.  Saved
persistent state is lost, excluding untitled documents.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-reveal
Select the named files in the Finder instead of opening them.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-wait-apps
Block until the applications that were used are closed, even if they were
already running.
.TP
.B \-\-args
Treat every remaining argument as an argument for the opened application's
.BR main (),
instead of as a file to open.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new
Open a new instance of the application even if one is already running.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-hide
Launch the application hidden.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-background
Do not bring the application to the foreground.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-H ", " \-\-header
Search header file locations for headers matching the given file names and
open them.  More than one matching header prompts for a choice.  Combine with
.B \-s
to restrict which SDKs are searched.  A name that matches no header is
reported as such.
.TP
.BI \-s " partial SDK name"
For
.BR \-h ,
search only those SDKs whose names contain this value.  Otherwise the
highest versioned SDK of each platform is used.
.TP
.BI \-u " URL" ", " \-\-url " URL"
Open this URL, even if it matches an existing file path exactly.
.TP
.BI \-i " path" ", " \-\-stdin " path"
Launch the application with its standard input connected to
.IR path ,
which defaults to /dev/null.
.TP
.BI \-o " path" ", " \-\-stdout " path"
Launch the application with its standard output connected to
.IR path .
.TP
.BI \-\-stderr " path"
Launch the application with its standard error connected to
.IR path .
.TP
.BI \-\-env " VAR"
Add an environment variable to the launched process, where
.I VAR
is formatted
.IR VAR = foo
or just
.I VAR
for a null string value.  May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-no-recents
Do not add the opened items to the recent items list.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B open
exits 0 on success, and 1 after a usage error or a failure to launch, such
as a named file that does not exist, an application that cannot be found, an
architecture that cannot be decoded, or a URL that no application claims.
The built-in help is printed, with the same status as a usage error, whenever
.B open
is given no file to open.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a document in its default application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open notes.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open it in a specific application, by name or by bundle identifier:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open -a Preview scan.tiff
open -b com.apple.Preview scan.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pass arguments to the opened application rather than opening files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
open -a Terminal --args -l
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B \-v
is accepted and has no effect.
.PP
There is no
.B \-help
option, and
.B \-x
is not an option: both are reported as invalid, as is any other unrecognized
spelling.  The help described above is the one printed when no file is named,
not a separate option.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pbcopy (1),
.BR pbpaste (1),
.BR textutil (1),
.BR tiffutil (1)
